## Architecture - the cross-platform build

Single module, so the top-level `CMakeLists.txt` does everything (unlike RMAP, which
splits a top-level file over four per-module `CMakeLists.txt`). Shape:

- `add_library(Map STATIC ${Map_FILES})` + `add_library(Map::Map ALIAS Map)`. Headers are
  listed in `Map_FILES` so they show in the VS project and get filtered by
  `source_group(TREE ...)`.
- `target_include_directories`: PUBLIC `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:include>` + `$<INSTALL_INTERFACE:
  include>` (so consumers and internal code use `<Map/Map.h>`); PRIVATE `include/Map` +
  `src` (mirrors RMAP core's PRIVATE `include/RMAP` + `src`: lets internal code include the
  public headers without the `Map/` prefix, and resolves `#include "pch.h"` / the PCH
  force-include from `src`). The PRIVATE `include/Map` was added when the headers moved
  under `include/Map/`.
- PCH via `target_precompile_headers(Map PRIVATE src/pch.h)` (mirrors RMAP core).
- `source_group(TREE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR} FILES ${Map_FILES})` -> filters
  `src`, `include` (disk-mirrored, same convention RMAP chose).
- `if(MSVC)`: `target_compile_definitions(... PRIVATE UNICODE _UNICODE _LIB
  _CRT_SECURE_NO_WARNINGS)` (dropped the old DLL defines `MVRPMAPCPP_EXPORTS` / `_USRDLL`;
  kept `_CRT_SECURE_NO_WARNINGS` because the old x64 configs used it),
  `target_compile_options(... /permissive- /sdl /W3)`, and
  `INTERPROCEDURAL_OPTIMIZATION_RELEASE ON`. This mirrors RMAP core's MSVC block.
- Install/export mirrors RMAP: `install(TARGETS Map EXPORT MapTargets ARCHIVE
  DESTINATION lib)`, `install(DIRECTORY include/ DESTINATION include)`,
  `install(EXPORT MapTargets NAMESPACE Map:: ...)`,
  `configure_package_config_file` + `write_basic_package_version_file` from
  `cmake/MapConfig.cmake.in`. Downstream: `find_package(Map CONFIG REQUIRED)` +
  `target_link_libraries(... Map::Map)`.

### KEY DECISION - static library, DLL glue dropped

Old project was `ConfigurationType=DynamicLibrary` producing `MVRP_Map.dll` (+ import
`.lib` + `.pdb`), Windows-only, with a `PostBuildEvent` copying outputs to `bin\`.
DavidSr chose (via the conversion questions) a **cross-platform STATIC library** and to
**drop the win32 DLL glue** - exactly what RMAP did. Consequently these files were
**deleted**: `src/win32/dllmain.cpp`, `src/win32/MVRP_Map.rc`, `src/win32/version.h`
(the .rc + dllmain were the only users of version.h), and the orphan `src/framework.h`
(only pulled `<windows.h>`); the now-empty `src/win32/` folder was removed.

**Export-macro (RESOLVED by DavidSr).** The old `MVRP_Map.h` defined `MV_MVRP_MAP_API` as
`__declspec(dllexport)` when `_WIN32 && MVRPMAPCPP_EXPORTS`, else `__declspec(dllimport)`
on `_WIN32`, else empty - wrong for a static lib. When DavidSr renamed/moved the header to
`include/Map/Map.h` (2026-07-27) he **removed the `__declspec` export block entirely** (and
the `MV_MVRP_MAP_API` annotations) - confirmed by grep: no `MV_MVRP_MAP_API`,
`MVRPMAPCPP_EXPORTS`, or `__declspec` remain in `Map.h`. So the static-lib API is now clean
with no CMake define needed (RMAP used `RMAP_CORE_API=`; Map needs no equivalent). The
`CMakeLists.txt` MSVC comment was updated to note the source's export macro was removed.

### KEY DECISION - RMAP is a COMPILED git third-party dependency (mirrors RMAP's own pattern)

DavidSr asked to "add RMAP as a third party library ... the same way RMAP added third
party libraries" (2026-07-27). RMAP's pattern for a COMPILED dep (BoringSSL/curl/sioclient)
is: an `option(<PROJ>_USE_SYSTEM_<DEP> ... OFF)`; OFF -> `ExternalProject_Add` that
git-clones + does a real CMake configure/build/install at BUILD time into a gitignored
`third_party/<dep>/`, consumed by referencing the installed static lib path +
`BUILD_BYPRODUCTS` (NOT `find_package`, because the artifact does not exist at configure
time); ON -> `find_package(<dep> CONFIG REQUIRED)`. Plus the `if(EXISTS "${dir}/.git")`
git-arg guard, `GIT_SHALLOW ON`, `UPDATE_DISCONNECTED ON`, and the `$<CONFIG>` genex only
in `CMAKE_ARGS`/`BUILD_BYPRODUCTS`/`--config`/target props (never in the ExternalProject
dir args).

Applied to Map (replacing the earlier `find_package(RMAP CONFIG QUIET)` scaffolding):
- `option(Map_USE_SYSTEM_RMAP ... OFF)`.
- OFF branch: `ExternalProject_Add(rmap_build ...)` clones
  `https://github.com/MetaversalCorp/RMAP.git` at `GIT_TAG main` (RMAP ships NO release
  tags yet - verified via `git tag` on the local clone; tracking `main` mirrors how RMAP
  tracks BoringSSL's `main`) into `third_party/rmap`, `BINARY_DIR
  ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/rmap/build`, installs to `Map_RMAP_PREFIX =
  ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR}/rmap/$<CONFIG>` via `--config $<CONFIG>`. Static lib path
  `Map_RMAP_LIB = ${Map_RMAP_PREFIX}/lib/${CMAKE_STATIC_LIBRARY_PREFIX}RMAP${CMAKE_STATIC_LIBRARY_SUFFIX}`
  (Windows `RMAP.lib`, Unix `libRMAP.a`) as `BUILD_BYPRODUCTS`. `add_dependencies(Map
  rmap_build)` so RMAP is installed before Map compiles. Consumption uses the
  `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:>` discipline: build-only PUBLIC include
  `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:${Map_RMAP_PREFIX}/include>` (so `<RMAP/...>` resolves) and PUBLIC
  link `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:${Map_RMAP_LIB}>` - both wrapped so the absolute build-tree
  path stays OUT of `MapTargets` (an installed Map resolves RMAP via `find_dependency`
  in `MapConfig.cmake` instead).
- ON branch: `find_package(RMAP CONFIG REQUIRED)` + `target_link_libraries(Map PUBLIC
  RMAP::RMAP)`.
- `MapConfig.cmake.in` now does an UNCONDITIONAL `find_dependency(RMAP)` (was guarded by
  `@RMAP_FOUND@`), because Map always links RMAP in both branches. `.gitignore` gained
  `/third_party/rmap/`.

Link is PUBLIC (not PRIVATE like RMAP's internal deps) because RMAP is Map's core framework
and appears throughout Map's headers/pch - analogous to how RMAP linked nlohmann_json
PUBLIC. CONSEQUENCE: building RMAP from source inherits RMAP's full build (its own
BoringSSL/curl/sioclient compiled deps) and toolchain (Go + NASM on Windows - confirmed
present on this machine per RMAP's notes: Go 1.26.2, NASM 3.01). Because RMAP.lib is a
static archive with its own deps linked PRIVATE, a final executable linking Map must also
link BoringSSL/curl/sioclient + their system libs (per RMAP's README). `Map_USE_SYSTEM_RMAP=ON`
skips the from-source build + toolchain by pointing at a prebuilt RMAP.

RMAP's full third-party set is mirrored into Map's `CMakeLists.txt` the SAME way RMAP
declares it (DavidSr: "do it the same way RMAP does, along with other libraries"). WHY: RMAP
exposes these through its public `<RMAP/...>` headers (e.g. `RMAP.h` pulls `nlohmann/json.hpp`
-> `ordered_json`) and Map's own SB/IO source uses them, but RMAP adds them only as
`$<BUILD_INTERFACE:>` (NOT installed), so a consumer like Map must supply them on its own
build path. Versions/tags/defines match RMAP exactly to avoid ABI skew across the shared API.
Each has a `Map_USE_SYSTEM_<NAME>` option (default OFF -> git fetch/build into
`third_party/<name>`; ON -> external copy). Two families:
- Header-only (fetched via ExternalProject with empty configure/build/install, added PUBLIC
  as `$<BUILD_INTERFACE:...>`): `nlohmann_json` v3.11.3 (`third_party/json/include`);
  `asio` `asio-1-30-2` (`third_party/asio/asio/include`, `+ASIO_STANDALONE` PUBLIC);
  `websocketpp` `0.8.2` (clone root, `+_WEBSOCKETPP_CPP11_STL_` PUBLIC).
- Compiled (real ExternalProject configure/build/install per `$<CONFIG>`, linked PRIVATE,
  build-only PUBLIC include): `boringssl` `main` (libssl/libcrypto; forwards a found `nasm`
  on Windows; +ws2_32/crypt32 on Win, +pthread/dl on Linux); `curl` `curl-8_9_1` (Schannel on
  Windows / BoringSSL elsewhere, `+CURL_STATICLIB` build-only, +Win system libs); `socketio`
  pinned to RMAP's master commit `3b7be7e...` (built as `sioclient_tls`, TLS via BoringSSL,
  MSVC EHsc/NOMINMAX/NOCRYPT flags, submodules). curl (off-Win) + sioclient
  `add_dependencies(... boringssl_build)`; each `add_dependencies(Map <target>)`. The
  compiled trio's ON branch reads `Map_SYSTEM_{BORINGSSL,CURL,SIOCLIENT}_ROOT` (PATH cache
  vars) - mirrors RMAP's `*_SYSTEM_*_ROOT`. `MapConfig.cmake.in` adds guarded
  `find_dependency` for json/asio/websocketpp (only fires when the corresponding
  `Map_USE_SYSTEM_*` was ON, i.e. their imported target is in Map's link interface). Verified:
  clean configure succeeds and generates all of asio_fetch/boringssl_build/curl_build/
  nlohmann_json_fetch/rmap_build/sioclient_build/websocketpp_fetch; built nlohmann_json_fetch
  to confirm `third_party/json/include/nlohmann/json.hpp` lands (fixes DavidSr's C1083). The
  heavy compiled builds (boringssl/curl/sioclient/rmap) were NOT run in this session.

For the three still-unconverted deps (MVRP, MVRP_Dev, MVXP) there is a
`Map_DEPENDENCY_INCLUDE_DIRS` cache STRING; when set it is added PRIVATE. Honest
scaffolding - it does not pretend those deps are resolved.

## Conventions

All Metaversal standards apply (see global `~/.cursor/rules/*.mdc`): 3-space indent,
Allman braces, Hungarian notation, single return, ALL-CAPS types/constants, Capital-first
function names, no tabs, no Python, symmetry (Rule 1). The `CMakeLists.txt`,
`CMakePresets.json`, `cmake/MapConfig.cmake.in`, `.gitignore`, and `README.md` were
written to mirror RMAP's equivalents as closely as the single-module shape allows
(symmetry across the two projects). CMake files use 3-space indentation matching RMAP.

Target naming: STATIC target `Map` (produces `Map.lib`), alias `Map::Map`, package `Map`,
export set `MapTargets`, config `MapConfig.cmake` - fully symmetric with RMAP. The old
MSVC `RootNamespace` was `MVRPMapcpp` and `TargetName` was `MVRP_Map`; the build-level
`MVRP_Map` name was dropped in favor of `Map`. The C++ source identifiers and header file
names remain `MVRP_Map` (source, pending migration).

